                author: "Rudyard Kipling",
                openLibraryId: "OL19908W",
                comment: "Kipling is a divisive figure, but I believe his genius is undeniable.  The strange lapidary prose of this book is more interesting and readable than the overpraised Moby-Dick or the largely turgid experiments of the so-called modernists.  Edward Said gives a pious summary of Kipling's thought crimes in the introduction to the Penguin edition.  Kipling, who believed he was inspired to write by a daimon, had a visionary power and creative gift that it would be a pity to deprive yourself of because you don't share his views.",
